{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c001", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c002", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The recital identifies Lender as \"the servicing agent and/or the owner and holder\" of the Note, leaving it undetermined in which capacity Aurora contracts and whether it holds authority to bind the noteholder. The disjunctive \"and/or\" makes the capacity, and hence the scope of Aurora's promises in paragraphs 3 and 6, indeterminate.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.27}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c003", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"[A] mortgage, deed of trust or comparable security unstrument\" defines the \"Security Instrument\" by alternatives without saying which instrument in fact secures the Note, and \"comparable\" has no stated boundary. Every later reference to the \"Security Instrument\" (paragraphs 6, 8, 9, 13) inherits that indefiniteness.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.13}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c004", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The Arrearage itemization lists \"Legal Fees\" as a separate line while the \"Corporate Advances\" definition states that Corporate Advances \"include\" legal fees and foreclosure fees, so the composition of the $13,755.30 total is open to a double-counting reading. What sums the fixed \"Arrearage\" figure actually comprises is therefore contestable.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.17}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c005", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The punctuation of \"principal, interest, and escrows, required, for taxes and insurance premium installments\" leaves it unclear whether escrows are a required component of PITI or only included where required. That matters because paragraph 13 contemplates that the loan may not be escrowed at all.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.12}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c006", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Corporate Advances\" is defined by an \"include, but are not limited to\" catch-all attached to an enumerated list of specific servicing fees, so what other charges may be swept into the Arrearage is unbounded. A court could be asked whether ejusdem generis confines the category to fees of the enumerated kind.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Other Fees\" is defined only by an open-ended \"include, but are not limited to\" formula listing short payment advances and Speed ACH fees, giving the residual category no ascertainable limit. The enumerated specifics invite an ejusdem generis argument about what else can be charged.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.18}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This recital states that Lender has already accelerated and \"may have already commenced foreclosure proceedings,\" which cannot be squared with the immediately following recital (c009) that Lender \"has not commenced Foreclosure proceedings\" and that no sale has been scheduled. Which recital states the actual status of the loan at execution is left to construction.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.38}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The recital that no foreclosure has been commenced and no sale scheduled conflicts with paragraph 4, which addresses \"postponements of a foreclosure sale,\" and paragraph 6, which speaks of \"resumption of a pending foreclosure action.\" A court would have to decide whether the recital or the operative paragraphs describe the parties' actual posture.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.35}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The recital that Lender \"has agreed to allow Customer to repay the Arrearage\" under the work-out arrangement conflicts with Attachment A paragraph b, which states the aggregate Plan payments will be insufficient to pay the Arrearage and that the account will remain delinquent. A borrower could argue the recital states the bargain's purpose and controls the operative payment schedule.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.40}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The no-personal-liability promise is limited to a Customer \"discharged in a Chapter 7 proceeding subsequent to the execution of the Loan Documents,\" saying nothing about discharges in other chapters or discharges granted before the Loan Documents. The expressio unius reading of that single enumerated circumstance is contestable, particularly against the cover letter's broader bankruptcy language.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c012", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Paragraph 2 makes the Agreement \"expire\" on the Attachment A Expiration Date, yet Attachment A paragraph b imposes duties \"[u]pon the Expiration Date\" (entering a Cure Method) that would arise only once the Agreement has expired. Whether any obligation survives expiration is left unresolved between the two.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c013", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The forbearance promise cannot be squared with paragraph 8, which states that a commenced foreclosure \"will not be withdrawn,\" and with paragraph 7's declaration that nothing herein waives any right to commence or resume foreclosure. What Lender actually gave up during the plan term is the very thing those provisions take back.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.42},
    {"issue": "\"[F]orbear from exercising any or all of its rights and remedies\" is ambiguous as to whether Lender must forbear from all remedies or may elect which ones to forbear from. The disjunctive \"any or all\" gives the borrower's principal protection no fixed content.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c014", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The admission that postponements of \"a foreclosure sale\" are by mutual consent presupposes a pending foreclosure, which the recital at c009 denies exists as of execution. A court would have to reconcile the admitted foreclosure posture with the recited one.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.26}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c015", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Paragraph 5 incorporates Attachment A wholesale without any order-of-precedence rule, leaving unresolved which text governs where the body and the Attachment differ (for example paragraph 15's \"Due Dates specified in Attachment A\" against Attachment A a.1's 11/18/2009 first-payment date). Incorporation alone does not say which controls.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c016", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Default\" is defined to include the Customer's failure \"to comply with any of the terms and conditions herein or therein,\" a catch-all of unbounded scope sitting beside the specific payment failures enumerated first. Whether trivial or non-monetary non-compliance triggers termination and foreclosure is left open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c017", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The blanket statement that \"[n]othing contained herein shall constitute a waiver of any of all of the Lender's rights or remedies, including the right to commence or resume foreclosure proceedings\" is irreconcilable with paragraph 3, in which Lender promises to forbear from exercising those very rights during the term. Read literally it drains the forbearance covenant of effect.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c018", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The clause that a commenced foreclosure \"will not be withdrawn unless Lender determines to do so by applicable law\" is grammatically incomplete and gives no ascertainable standard for when withdrawal occurs. Whether \"by applicable law\" qualifies Lender's determination or the withdrawal itself changes the borrower's position materially.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.25}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c019", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Except as otherwise provided in this Agreement\" ratifies the Loan Documents without identifying which of their terms this Agreement displaces, a question paragraph 15 (disapplying the Loan Documents' grace period) and Attachment A's payment schedule squarely raise. Which instrument governs a given payment obligation is left to construction.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c020", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The footnote's example — Lender accepting a partial payment \"instead of returning such payment\" — sits against paragraph 10, which directs that under-payments be returned or held in suspense at Lender's sole option and never refunded. Whether acceptance of a partial payment is governed by the footnote or by paragraph 10 is unclear.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.18}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c021", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Applying payments \"first to the earliest monthly payment under the Note that is due\" cannot be squared with Attachment A a.1, which states the stipulated payments are made \"[f]or purposes of repayment of the Arrearage.\" Whether Plan payments reduce the Arrearage or are absorbed by ongoing Note installments is left contradictory.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
  ]}
]}

